DB::open indexes a hint-less data file without making it durable first:
no rewrite, and the tail trimmed with a plain resize_file, as before #240. A
process killed with unsynced writes leaves them in the page cache; the next
open writes a durable hint over them, and a power cut then takes the bytes
the hint points at. Reverts #240's open half (#231).
Expected: caught.
Guarded-by: "open makes a hint-less file durable before it indexes it: a process killed before its sync"

diff --git a/bytecaskdb/db_recovery.cpp b/bytecaskdb/db_recovery.cpp
index 87382b4..7767f59 100644
--- a/bytecaskdb/db_recovery.cpp
+++ b/bytecaskdb/db_recovery.cpp
@@ -229,7 +229,6 @@ auto DB::recovery_prepare_files(EngineState &s)
     // durable before anything reads them — every hint-less file, since none
     // of them says which one was active.
     // No writer says where its entries end, so the whole file.
-    if (hintless) rewrite_durably(p, std::filesystem::file_size(p));
     auto data_file =
         openDataFileForRead(p, io_backend_, pool_, file_id);
 
@@ -246,7 +245,7 @@ auto DB::recovery_prepare_files(EngineState &s)
       flush_hints_for(data_file, dir_, [&](Offset end) {
         recovery_check_tail(*data_file, end, data_paths);
         if (end < data_file->size()) {
-          truncate_durably(p, end);
+          std::filesystem::resize_file(p, end);
           cut = true;
         }
       });
